#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Boot an ISO in QEMU and check every frame the screen shows, up to the greeter.

The live ISO draws the Omarchy logo on the console as the initramfs starts,
where the installer's greeter will draw it, and the greeter draws over it in
place: from the first logo on, the screen should only ever show that logo,
the greeter, and the dark of a mode switch. This boots the ISO unmodified, as
a throttled USB stick through its firmware and boot loader like the boot
benchmark, saves every frame that differs from the one before, and checks
each one:

- the logo, cell by cell: logo.txt drawn at the console's cell size (8x16 or
  16x32 on a framebuffer, the half-size logo in 9x16 cells on the VGA text
  console), every half cell either inked or empty as the logo has it, which
  box drawing, a half-drawn or half-erased logo, or another font all fail;
- its place: the greeter's (console-logo.sh and the configurator lay it out
  the same way), and the same place in every frame of the same size;
- nothing else lit: no kernel or login text, no copy of the logo, and on the
  greeter only the tagline and hint, centred, on their rows;
- no dark frames between the first logo and the greeter longer than
  --max-dark, and none after the greeter;
- no text between the boot loader's last dark frame and the first logo.

Frames go to OUT/frames as PNG, named by their time since QEMU started and
what they showed; OUT/report.txt has the timeline and the problems. Exit 0
when there are none, 1 when there are, 2 when the run itself failed.

The logo it expects is the one the ISO under test draws: the greeter's and
the copy fitted to 80 columns, read out of its live initramfs
(console-logo/greeter.txt, fitted.txt) and kept in OUT/logo. On a console
narrower than both (a 600-pixel-wide framebuffer's 75 columns) the fitted one
is drawn from its first column and cut at its last.

Usage: test/boot-render ISO [options]
       test/boot-render --analyze OUT [--logo PATH]

  --firmware uefi|bios  (default uefi)
  --device DEV          QEMU display device (default virtio-vga): VGA,
                        virtio-vga, virtio-gpu-pci, bochs-display,
                        cirrus-vga, qxl-vga, vmware-svga, ati-vga, ramfb
  --resolution WxH      The display's EDID resolution (VGA, virtio-vga,
                        virtio-gpu-pci and bochs-display; default 1280x800)
  --usb usb2|usb3       The stick: EHCI at 40 MB/s or xHCI at 150 MB/s
                        (default usb3)
  --fps N               Screendumps a second (default 25)
  --timeout SECS        Give up without a greeter after this long (default 180)
  --after-greeter SECS  Keep watching the greeter this long (default 2)
  --max-dark SECS       Longest dark stretch allowed after the first logo
                        (default 0.5)
  --out DIR             (default test-runs/<iso>-boot-render/<firmware>-<device>-<resolution>)
  --logo PATH           The greeter's logo, in place of the ISO's (where that
                        cannot be read: /usr/share/omarchy/logo.txt)
  --cpu MODEL           QEMU's -cpu (default host,ucode-rev=0xffffffff: KVM
                        loads no microcode, and a Zen 5 guest with the host's
                        old revision prints "RDSEED32 is broken" at emergency
                        level, where a real machine has the ISO's early
                        microcode update first)
  --journal             Also log in over SSH once the greeter is up (as the
                        boot benchmark does) and save the guest's journal and
                        kernel log to OUT/journal.txt (the guest gets a NIC)
  --qemu-arg ARG        One more argument for QEMU, repeatable: a systemd
                        credential through -smbios type=11, say, to try a
                        change without a rebuild
"""

